        'Not necessarily, though it\'s worth knowing upfront that sources genuinely disagree on exactly how likely a vaginal birth is next time. Large international reviews put success at around 60 to 80 percent for women who attempt a vaginal birth after caesarean (VBAC). At least one Indian tertiary hospital study found a considerably lower real-world figure, closer to 48 percent. Both numbers are legitimate; they simply reflect different patient groups and different hospital settings.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Why the figures differ so much: success depends heavily on why your first C-section was needed, whether you\'ve ever delivered vaginally before, your BMI, and whether labour starts on its own rather than being induced. International reviews often pool data from hospitals with strong, established support for attempting labour after a caesarean. Some Indian hospital studies reflect settings where a repeat C-section is chosen sooner if labour isn\'t progressing quickly, which pulls the measured real-world success rate down. Neither figure is wrong; they\'re genuinely measuring somewhat different things.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: rather than anchoring on one statistic you found online, ask your own doctor what they\'d realistically expect for you, based on your specific history and their hospital\'s approach, once you\'re actually expecting again. One figure is worth holding onto regardless of which success rate applies to you: the risk of the earlier scar tearing during labour, called uterine rupture, stays low either way, roughly 3 to 7 in 1,000 attempts. A caution worth stating plainly: attempting a vaginal birth after a caesarean should only happen at a hospital equipped to perform an emergency C-section immediately if needed. This isn\'t optional context; it\'s a genuine safety requirement, not a detail to skip.',
