  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-088',
    number: 315,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What is perineal massage, and does it actually help avoid tearing?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (from weeks 34-35)',
    canI: 'Do',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Perineal massage is gently stretching and massaging the area between your vagina and anus, usually with a natural oil, starting from around 34 to 35 weeks. The evidence on how well it works is genuinely mixed: some reviews show a real benefit for first-time mothers, while at least one large trial found no significant difference.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'A Cochrane review pooling several trials found that perineal massage in the last weeks of pregnancy reduced the likelihood of trauma requiring stitches, with the benefit clearest for women having their first vaginal birth. Against this, a large randomised trial of over 1,000 women found no significant difference in tearing or episiotomy rates between women who did it and those who didn\'t. Both are legitimate findings; the honest picture is that the overall evidence leans modestly positive without being conclusive.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: it\'s a low-risk, low-cost technique worth trying if you\'re comfortable with it, particularly if this is your first vaginal birth, but don\'t treat it as a guarantee against tearing. If you\'d like to try it, doing it two to three times a week from around 34 to 35 weeks, using a plain, unscented oil, is the pattern most studies have used.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor or a physiotherapist to show you the technique if you\'re unsure, and mention it if you have any pregnancy complications that might make it inadvisable.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth naming plainly: no technique, including this one, can promise a tear-free birth. What it may do is modestly improve your odds, which is worth something without being a guarantee.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Perineal massage means gently stretching the area from around 34 to 35 weeks.',
        'Evidence is genuinely mixed; a Cochrane review found benefit, a large trial found none.',
        'The overall evidence leans modestly positive without being conclusive.',
        'It\'s low-risk and worth trying, especially for a first vaginal birth.',
        'It\'s not a guarantee against tearing, whatever the outcome.',
      ]),
    ],
