  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-089',
    number: 316,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Can I request a C-section without a medical reason (maternal-request C-section) in India?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (delivery planning)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (ethically live topic in Indian obstetrics, per Escalation Guardrail)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Technically, some private hospitals in India will perform a caesarean section (C-section) at a woman\'s request even without a medical reason, though it isn\'t standard practice and major medical bodies don\'t recommend it as a routine option. The honest picture: there are real arguments on both sides of this, and it\'s worth understanding both before deciding.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'The case for allowing it rests on patient autonomy, your right to make informed decisions about your own body, and it can genuinely help some women who have a real, deep fear of vaginal birth, often after a difficult previous experience. The case against it rests on safety data: a large World Health Organization survey found that a C-section performed without a medical reason carried a meaningfully higher risk of serious complications, including infection, blood transfusion, and hysterectomy, compared with a straightforward vaginal birth. The World Health Organization\'s broader position is that C-section rates above 10 to 15 percent don\'t further improve outcomes for mothers or babies at a population level.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: this is a decision to make with your doctor rather than alone, and it\'s worth exploring why you\'d prefer this option first. If fear of labour or a previous difficult birth is driving the request, addressing that fear directly, through counselling, a supportive birth plan, or pain relief options like an epidural, may serve you better than surgery chosen to avoid it. If, after that conversation, you still prefer a planned C-section, most private hospitals in India will discuss it with you directly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Raise this honestly with your doctor well before your due date, including what\'s actually driving the preference, so they can genuinely help you think it through.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth stating plainly: this is your decision to make with informed medical guidance, not something to decide from anxiety alone; the fear underneath the request often deserves its own attention first.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Some private Indian hospitals will discuss a C-section without a medical reason.',
        'Major medical bodies don\'t recommend it as a routine option.',
        'Research shows a meaningfully higher complication risk versus an uncomplicated vaginal birth.',
        'Fear of labour, if that\'s driving the request, is worth addressing directly first.',
        'This is a decision to make with your doctor, not alone.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-081',
