  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-090',
    number: 317,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What is External Cephalic Version (ECV), and how is it actually done?',
    category: 'Baby Development',
    applicability: 'Month 8 to 9 (weeks 36-37 typically)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'External Cephalic Version (ECV) is a procedure where your doctor attempts to gently turn your baby from a breech (bottom-down) position to head-down, by applying firm pressure on your belly with their hands. It\'s usually offered around 36 to 37 weeks and is done in a hospital setting, with your baby monitored closely throughout.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Your doctor uses both hands, one to gently lift your baby\'s bottom, the other to guide their head downward, coaxing them through a slow, forward roll. This is done with continuous monitoring of your baby\'s heartbeat before, during, and after, and always in a hospital where an emergency caesarean section (C-section) could be performed immediately if needed, since that safety net is part of how the procedure is kept safe.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'The procedure itself typically takes just a few minutes, though it can feel like firm, sustained pressure, sometimes uncomfortable rather than painful for most women. Success rates vary considerably depending on your specific situation, roughly 50 to 65 percent overall, and are generally higher if you\'ve given birth vaginally before.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether you\'re a suitable candidate for the procedure and what your personal chances of success might be, based on your specific pregnancy.',
