  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-091',
    number: 318,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Are medications used during External Cephalic Version (ECV) to help turn the breech baby?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 to 9 (at time of ECV, typically weeks 36-37)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Often, yes. A medication that relaxes your womb\'s muscle, sometimes given as an injection shortly before the procedure, is commonly used to improve the chances of External Cephalic Version (ECV) succeeding. Your doctor will discuss whether this applies to you and explain the specific medication and dose, which isn\'t something to research or decide on your own.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'A relaxed womb gives your baby more room to be guided into a head-down position, which is exactly why this type of medication measurably improves success rates in research studies, generally by around a third compared with attempting the procedure without it. This is a well-established, commonly used approach in External Cephalic Version (ECV) internationally, not an experimental one.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If this medication is recommended for you, your doctor will explain any relevant precautions based on your health history, since it isn\'t suitable for everyone. Mild, temporary side effects like a faster heartbeat or slight shakiness are possible and expected; they pass quickly and are closely monitored.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor directly whether this medication will be part of your specific procedure, and what to expect from it.',
