  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-092',
    number: 319,
    month: 8,
    question: 'If External Cephalic Version (ECV) doesn\'t work or isn\'t suitable for turning my breech baby, what are my options?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 to 9 (around weeks 36-37)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (delivery-mode decision content, per Escalation Guardrail)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'This FAQ is for a breech baby, one positioned bottom or feet-down rather than head-down. If External Cephalic Version (ECV), the procedure to gently turn a breech baby by hand, isn\'t successful, or isn\'t suitable for you in the first place, your doctor will most often recommend a planned caesarean section (C-section). In carefully selected cases, a vaginal breech birth may be an option at some hospitals, though this is less commonly offered and depends heavily on your specific situation and your hospital\'s experience with it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Most hospitals, including most in India, plan a C-section for a baby who remains breech at term, since research comparing the two options for breech birth generally shows better outcomes for the baby with a planned C-section. A small number of specialist centres do offer supervised vaginal breech birth for well-selected candidates, but this requires specific expertise that isn\'t available everywhere.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If External Cephalic Version (ECV) doesn\'t work, ask your doctor directly what their hospital\'s standard approach is, and whether a vaginal breech birth is genuinely an option there. If it isn\'t, that reflects your hospital\'s resources and experience rather than something being wrong with your specific pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask this before attempting External Cephalic Version (ECV), so you already know your hospital\'s plan either way, rather than facing a new decision immediately afterward.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth naming plainly: this isn\'t a case where one option is simply "better" in the abstract; it depends heavily on what your specific hospital can safely support, which is exactly why asking in advance matters.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A planned C-section is the most common recommendation if a baby stays breech.',
        'Vaginal breech birth is possible at some specialist centres, for well-selected cases.',
        'Research generally shows better outcomes for planned C-section in this situation.',
        'Ask your hospital\'s standard approach before attempting External Cephalic Version (ECV).',
        'This depends on your specific hospital\'s resources and experience.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-090',
      'PREG-HEALTH-080',
      'PREG-HEALTH-078',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-PARTNER-007',
    number: 320,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Can family members, like my mother or mother-in-law, be present in the delivery room?',
    category: 'Partner & Relationships',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (hospital and birth planning)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Most hospitals, whether government or private, allow only one birth companion in the labour or delivery room, and it\'s usually your choice who that is. If you\'d like your mother or mother-in-law present, rather than or alongside a partner, this is worth discussing with your hospital directly, since policies genuinely vary.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'The government\'s 2016 birth companion policy for public hospitals was actually written around this exact question, allowing the woman\'s choice of companion, historically encouraged to be a female relative with labour experience, though implementation still varies by state and facility. Private hospitals generally allow one support person too, and while it\'s often assumed to be the husband or partner, most hospitals genuinely leave this choice to you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Think about who you\'d genuinely want beside you, since it doesn\'t have to default to your partner if someone else would support you better in that moment. Confirm with your hospital whether it\'s strictly one person, or whether some flexibility exists, particularly for a longer labour versus the delivery moment itself.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Raise this with your hospital\'s maternity ward directly during a prenatal visit or tour, alongside your questions about partner presence.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There\'s no single "right" companion for this moment; it\'s genuinely your choice, and most hospitals are more flexible about who than people assume.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Most hospitals allow one birth companion, and it\'s usually your choice.',
        'Government hospitals have policy support for a companion of your choosing since 2016.',
