  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-093',
    number: 321,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What does "waters breaking" actually mean, and what is the amniotic sac?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'The amniotic sac is the fluid-filled bag that has surrounded and cushioned your baby throughout pregnancy. "Waters breaking" means this sac tears, medically called rupture of membranes, releasing the fluid inside, usually as labour begins or gets underway.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'This thin, tough sac has held your baby safely in fluid since very early in pregnancy, cushioning them from bumps and pressure, and helping their lungs and digestive system develop. As labour approaches, the sac naturally weakens and eventually tears, either on its own or sometimes deliberately by your doctor if labour needs a gentle nudge.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There\'s genuinely no single way this looks or feels; some women experience a sudden, noticeable gush, others a slow, steady trickle that continues for a while. Both are your waters breaking; neither is more "real" than the other.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Call your doctor or your hospital\'s maternity unit as soon as you suspect your waters have broken, whatever it looked or felt like.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This particular sign gets built up by films and stories into one dramatic moment; in reality it\'s simply your baby\'s cushion doing exactly what it\'s meant to, right on schedule.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The amniotic sac is the fluid-filled bag that has cushioned your baby throughout pregnancy.',
        '"Waters breaking" means this sac tears, releasing the fluid inside.',
        'It usually happens as labour begins or gets underway.',
        'It can be a sudden gush or a slow, steady trickle; both are normal.',
        'Call your doctor as soon as you suspect it\'s happened.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-094',
      'PREG-HEALTH-085',
      'PREG-HEALTH-096',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-094',
    number: 322,
    month: 8,
    question: 'How can I tell if my waters have actually broken, versus urine leakage or discharge?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'It can genuinely be hard to tell, especially with a slow trickle, and that\'s completely normal to be unsure about. A few clues help: amniotic fluid is usually clear or pale, often odourless or slightly sweet smelling, and it tends to keep leaking rather than stopping, unlike urine.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Urine leakage is extremely common in late pregnancy, since your baby\'s weight presses on your bladder, and it can genuinely feel similar to a small trickle of fluid. Amniotic fluid, by contrast, usually continues rather than being a one-off leak, and doesn\'t have urine\'s characteristic smell or colour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you\'re unsure, don\'t try to work it out alone at home; call your doctor or hospital regardless. A simple test done at the hospital, checking the fluid with a specific strip or under a microscope, can confirm within minutes whether it\'s amniotic fluid, so there\'s no need to feel certain before calling.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Call as soon as you suspect it, even if you\'re genuinely unsure; being wrong costs nothing, and a hospital visit for reassurance is completely reasonable.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Uncertainty here is the rule, not the exception; nearly every woman has this exact moment of "is this it?", and calling to check is always the right move.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Amniotic fluid is usually clear or pale, often odourless or slightly sweet.',
        'Urine leakage in late pregnancy is common and can feel similar.',
        'Amniotic fluid tends to keep leaking rather than being a one-off trickle.',
        'A simple hospital test can confirm the difference within minutes.',
        'Call regardless of certainty; there\'s no downside to checking.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-093',
      'PREG-HEALTH-085',
      'PREG-BODY-061',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-095',
    number: 323,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What if my waters break but contractions don\'t start on their own?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'This happens to some women, and your care team will monitor you closely and discuss next steps, usually within 24 hours. Options typically include waiting a short while for labour to start naturally or starting induction, and your doctor will explain which makes sense for you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Once your waters have broken, the protective barrier around your baby is open, which is why doctors watch more closely for signs of infection the longer labour takes to start on its own. This is exactly why there\'s a fairly standard window, commonly around 24 hours, after which most doctors will recommend actively starting labour if it hasn\'t begun.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You\'ll likely be monitored in the hospital during this waiting period, including checks on your temperature and your baby\'s heartbate, watching for any sign that waiting isn\'t the safest choice anymore. If labour doesn\'t start within the window your doctor recommends, induction is a well-established, safe next step, not a sign anything has gone wrong.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor what their specific approach and timeline is once you\'re at the hospital, so you know what to expect.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Bodies don\'t all follow the same script, even after waters break; your care team\'s job here is simply to keep watching closely and step in with support exactly when it\'s needed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Some women\'s contractions don\'t start right away after their waters break.',
        'Your care team monitors closely and typically acts within about 24 hours.',
        'The longer labour takes to start, the more infection risk is watched for.',
        'Induction after this window is a standard, safe next step.',
        'You\'ll be monitored, including temperature and your baby\'s heartbeat, while waiting.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-085',
      'PREG-HEALTH-086',
      'PREG-HEALTH-096',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-096',
    number: 324,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What happens if my waters break before 37 weeks (called preterm premature rupture of membranes)?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 (before 37 weeks specifically)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (preterm/emergency clinical content, per Escalation Guardrail)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'This is called preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM), and it needs prompt hospital assessment. Your care team will balance the benefits of your baby staying in the womb a little longer against the small but real risk of infection once your waters have broken.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Every extra week inside genuinely helps your baby\'s lungs and other organs mature, which is why doctors often try to delay delivery when it\'s safe to do so, sometimes with medication to support your baby\'s lung development in the meantime. But the open protective barrier does raise infection risk over time, which is why this balance is watched extremely closely, day by day, rather than left to run its course.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You\'ll likely be admitted to hospital for monitoring, which may include regular checks on your temperature, your baby\'s heartbeat, and blood tests watching for early signs of infection. Your doctor will explain the specific plan for your gestational age and situation, since the approach differs depending on how early this is happening.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Go to the hospital immediately if you suspect your waters have broken before 37 weeks; this isn\'t something to wait on.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This diagnosis understandably feels frightening, but it comes with a genuinely well-established, closely monitored path forward, built specifically around keeping both you and your baby as safe as possible.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Waters breaking before 37 weeks is called preterm premature rupture of membranes.',
        'It needs prompt hospital assessment, not waiting at home.',
        'Doctors balance more time for your baby\'s lungs against infection risk.',
        'You\'ll likely be admitted for close monitoring.',
        'The specific plan depends on exactly how early this is happening.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-117',
      'PREG-HEALTH-066',
      'PREG-HEALTH-118',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-097',
    number: 325,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Do dates really help prepare for labour, and how should I eat them?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (weeks 36+)',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'The research is more promising than for most "natural labour" suggestions, though still based on a handful of small studies. A few clinical trials found that eating around 70 to 75 grams of dates daily, roughly 6 to 8 dates, from about 36 to 37 weeks was linked to better cervical readiness, shorter labour, and fewer inductions.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Researchers think dates may work in a couple of ways: they may help your body respond better to a labour hormone called oxytocin, and they\'re rich in natural sugars and nutrients that support energy in the final weeks regardless. It\'s worth being honest about the limits here too; the trials involved relatively small numbers of women, mostly in specific countries, so the findings, while genuinely promising, aren\'t yet considered fully conclusive.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: eating dates daily from around 36 weeks is a reasonable, low-risk thing to try, especially since dates are already a familiar, welcome food in many Indian households. Aim for roughly 6 to 8 dates a day if you\'d like to try it, but mention it to your doctor if you have gestational diabetes, since dates do affect blood sugar and your intake may need adjusting.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention this to your doctor if you have gestational diabetes or any concerns about blood sugar, before making dates a daily habit.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth stating plainly: promising isn\'t the same as guaranteed; dates are a genuinely reasonable thing to try, not a reliable way to control when labour starts.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Around 6 to 8 dates daily from 36 to 37 weeks shows the most promising research.',
        'Linked to better cervical readiness, shorter labour, and fewer inductions in trials.',
        'Studies are still relatively small, so findings are promising but not conclusive.',
        'A reasonable, low-risk food to try, especially given its place in Indian diets.',
        'Mention it to your doctor if you have gestational diabetes.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-086',
      'PREG-HEALTH-084',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-014',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-098',
    number: 326,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What is an epidural, and how is it actually administered, step by step?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (birth planning)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'An epidural is placed by an anaesthetist through a thin tube inserted into your lower back, and the whole process, from positioning to pain relief taking effect, usually takes around 20 to 30 minutes. You\'ll stay awake and alert throughout; only the area from roughly your bellybutton to your upper legs goes numb.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'You\'ll be asked to sit or lie curled on your side, keeping still while your back is cleaned and numbed with a local anaesthetic, which causes a brief sting. A needle is then placed between the bones of your lower spine, a thin catheter (a small flexible tube) is threaded through it, and the needle itself is removed, leaving only the soft catheter taped to your back. Medication then flows through this catheter continuously, topped up or adjusted as needed for the rest of your labour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Most women feel pressure rather than sharp pain during placement, since the area is numbed first. You\'ll likely need a drip in your arm for fluids, and sometimes a thin tube to help empty your bladder, since the numbness can make this harder to do yourself. You can usually request an epidural at any point during labour once you\'re in a hospital that offers it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your anaesthetist to explain exactly what to expect before the procedure starts, including how long it takes to work and how it will be adjusted through labour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The mechanics can sound intimidating on paper; in practice, most women describe it as a brief, manageable moment followed by real, lasting relief.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Placement takes about 20 to 30 minutes; pain relief follows shortly after.',
        'Your back is numbed first, so most women feel pressure, not sharp pain.',
        'A thin catheter stays taped to your back, delivering medication continuously.',
        'You\'ll likely need an arm drip and sometimes a bladder catheter.',
        'You can usually request one at any point during labour.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-087',
      'PREG-HEALTH-099',
      'PREG-HEALTH-100',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-099',
    number: 327,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Does an epidural cause backache, harm the baby, or increase the chance of a C-section?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (birth planning)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'No, no, and no, these are three of the most persistent epidural myths, and the research consistently doesn\'t support any of them. A large Cochrane review found no significant link between epidurals and long-term back pain, no meaningful harm to the baby, and no increase in the chance of needing a C-section.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'The backache myth likely persists because labour and delivery themselves commonly cause back pain, from pushing, from loosened ligaments, from the mechanical strain of birth, regardless of whether you had an epidural. Any soreness right at the injection site is real but temporary, usually settling within days. On the baby: only a very small amount of medication crosses to your baby, and there\'s no evidence it causes harm. On C-section rates: multiple large reviews have found epidurals don\'t increase this risk; what they can do is modestly lengthen the second stage of labour and slightly raise the chance of an assisted vaginal delivery, using forceps or vacuum, which is a real, separate consideration worth knowing about.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You can weigh an epidural on its actual evidence rather than on these specific myths, which shouldn\'t factor into your decision either way. The genuinely relevant trade-off is the slightly longer second stage and modestly higher assisted-delivery rate, not the backache or C-section myths.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor about the real trade-offs relevant to your decision, rather than the myths, if you\'re still deciding.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It\'s worth separating what\'s actually uncertain from what simply isn\'t; on these three specific points, the evidence is genuinely settled.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'No significant link between epidurals and long-term back pain.',
        'No meaningful harm to your baby from the small amount of medication that crosses over.',
        'No increase in the chance of needing a C-section.',
        'A real trade-off does exist: a somewhat longer second stage and higher assisted-delivery chance.',
        'Weigh your decision on the actual evidence, not persistent myths.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-098',
      'PREG-HEALTH-100',
      'PREG-HEALTH-101',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-100',
    number: 328,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Who shouldn\'t have an epidural, and what happens if I can\'t have one?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (birth planning)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'An epidural isn\'t suitable if you have a bleeding or blood clotting disorder, a low platelet count, an infection at or near the injection site, or in certain other specific situations your doctor will check for. If it\'s not an option for you, other effective pain relief methods exist, and you\'ll receive support to manage labour safely either way.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'These restrictions exist because inserting a needle near your spine carries specific risks that are meaningfully higher if your blood doesn\'t clot normally or if there\'s infection nearby. Your anaesthetist will review your health history and recent blood tests before the procedure specifically to check for these factors, which is a routine, expected part of the process, not a sign of a problem.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If an epidural isn\'t suitable for you, options like intravenous pain medication, nitrous oxide gas where available, and non-medication approaches such as breathing techniques, massage, and movement can all help manage labour pain. Ask your specific hospital what they offer, since availability of some of these varies across India.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor early, during a third-trimester visit, whether anything in your health history might make an epidural unsuitable, so you\'re not learning this for the first time during labour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Not being a candidate for an epidural doesn\'t mean facing labour without support; it simply means a different, equally valid pain management plan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Bleeding disorders, low platelets, and nearby infection can rule out an epidural.',
        'Your anaesthetist checks your history and blood tests beforehand.',
        'This is a routine safety check, not a sign anything is wrong.',
