        'Options include intravenous or injected pain medication, nitrous oxide gas (sometimes called laughing gas), and non-medication approaches like warm water immersion, massage, movement, and breathing techniques. Not every option is available at every Indian hospital, so it\'s genuinely worth asking in advance.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Injected opioid medication can take the edge off pain and is sometimes used earlier in labour, though it can cause drowsiness or nausea and works differently from an epidural, since it doesn\'t numb the area. Nitrous oxide is self-administered through a mask, breathed in as a contraction begins, and its effects wear off quickly once you stop; it\'s well established internationally but far less commonly available in India than an epidural. Non-medication methods, a warm shower or bath, changing positions, massage, and focused breathing, don\'t remove pain the way medication does, but many women find them genuinely helpful, particularly in earlier labour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Ask your specific hospital what\'s actually available, since options vary considerably, and don\'t assume nitrous oxide or water immersion will be offered just because you\'ve read about them. It\'s also completely normal to combine approaches, starting with non-medication methods and adding medication later if needed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your hospital directly what pain relief options they offer, ideally during a prenatal visit rather than assuming.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There\'s no single "right" way to manage labour pain; the right combination is simply the one that\'s actually available to you and feels right in the moment.',
