    canI: 'Do',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Legally, yes, it\'s mandatory. Under India\'s Central Motor Vehicles Rules, children must be properly restrained in a car, which in practice means a car seat for a newborn. In reality, though, awareness and enforcement are both genuinely weak, and most Indian families still don\'t use one, which doesn\'t make it any less worth buying.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'A properly fitted car seat meaningfully reduces the risk of serious injury to an infant in a crash, and standard adult seatbelts and airbags simply aren\'t designed to protect a baby\'s body. Despite this, one survey found the large majority of Indian parents were unaware car seats for children existed or had never used one, and few hospitals actively raise it at discharge, so this isn\'t something you can count on being reminded about.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: buy and correctly install a rear-facing infant car seat before your due date, ideally by around 36 weeks, rather than treating it as optional or something to arrange later. Look for one certified to the Indian AIS-072 safety standard, and have it installed and ready in your car in advance, since you don\'t want to be figuring this out on the day you\'re discharged.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'This isn\'t a medical question, but you can ask your hospital whether they check for one at discharge, since practices vary.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth stating plainly: "most families don\'t do this" isn\'t the same as "it\'s not necessary." This is genuinely one of the more overlooked safety steps in Indian new-parent preparation.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Indian law requires children to be properly restrained in a car.',
        'Enforcement and awareness remain genuinely weak across India.',
        'A rear-facing infant car seat is the standard, correct choice for a newborn.',
        'Look for AIS-072 certification, the Indian safety standard.',
        'Buy and install it before your due date, ideally by week 36.',
      ]),
    ],
