    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, generally safe and genuinely helpful, with some sensible modifications as your bump grows. In the third trimester, the focus shifts from building strength to gentle stretching, hip and pelvic opening, and breathing practice that can genuinely help during labour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Gentle, guided yoga in these final months can ease common discomforts like back pain and pelvic pressure, while also helping you feel calmer and more prepared for labour. A few things are worth avoiding now specifically: deep twists, lying flat on your back or stomach for extended periods, and any pose that risks losing your balance, since your changed centre of gravity makes falls more likely than earlier in pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A certified prenatal instructor, or a class specifically designed for the third trimester, is worth seeking out rather than continuing an earlier-pregnancy routine unchanged, since what felt fine at 20 weeks may not suit you now. Shorter sessions, around 20 to 30 minutes, two to three times a week, with props like bolsters and blocks for support, tend to work well at this stage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
