  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-106',
    number: 335,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Why is a Group B Streptococcus (GBS) test done in late pregnancy, and is it routine in India?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'Month 8 to 9 (weeks 35-37)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Group B Streptococcus (GBS) is a common bacterium that usually causes no problems for you, but can occasionally pass to your baby during a vaginal birth. Internationally, testing everyone between 35 and 37 weeks is standard practice in many countries; honestly, it isn\'t a universal requirement in India, where doctors more often test based on individual risk factors rather than testing every pregnant woman.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'The test itself is simple, a swab of your vagina and rectum, taking less than a minute and causing no pain, with results usually available within a couple of days. Where the international approach and Indian practice genuinely differ: countries with universal screening test every pregnant woman at 35 to 37 weeks regardless of risk factors, while Indian guidance leans toward testing specifically when there\'s a higher-risk situation, like labour starting early or a previous baby affected by this infection. Overall reported rates of this infection appear lower in Indian studies than in Western ones, though it does still cause real illness in newborns here.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Ask your doctor directly whether they test for this routinely or based on risk factors, since practice genuinely varies between hospitals. If you test positive, or if risk factors apply to you, you\'ll typically receive antibiotics through an intravenous drip once labour begins, which very effectively lowers the chance of passing the infection to your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor around 34 to 36 weeks whether Group B Streptococcus testing is part of your specific care plan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This is a case where "not routinely tested for" doesn\'t mean "not taken seriously"; it reflects a genuinely different, risk-based approach to the same underlying concern.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Group B Streptococcus (GBS) is common and usually harmless, but can affect a newborn.',
        'Many countries test every pregnant woman at 35 to 37 weeks; India doesn\'t have this mandate.',
        'Indian practice leans toward testing based on individual risk factors.',
        'The test itself is a simple, painless swab with results in a couple of days.',
        'Antibiotics during labour very effectively lower the risk if you test positive.',
      ]),
    ],
