        'This is melasma, sometimes called chloasma or "the pregnancy mask," dark, symmetrical patches usually on your forehead, cheeks, nose, or upper lip. It\'s caused by pregnancy hormones and is completely harmless, though genuinely common, affecting a large share of pregnant women, especially those with more melanin in their skin.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Rising estrogen and progesterone stimulate the cells that produce your skin\'s pigment, and sun exposure makes this considerably more pronounced, which is exactly why patches often darken further with time outdoors. Women with darker skin tones, which includes most Indian skin types, are genuinely more prone to melasma, since these cells are naturally more reactive to begin with.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Daily sunscreen and a wide-brimmed hat when you\'re outdoors are the most useful things you can do right now, since sun exposure is the biggest factor within your control. Most active melasma treatments, prescription creams, peels, laser therapy, aren\'t considered safe during pregnancy, so treatment is best left until after you\'ve delivered and finished breastfeeding, when a dermatologist can guide you properly.',
