    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'If your doctor believes you\'re at genuine risk of delivering within the next week, a course of injections may be given to help your baby\'s lungs mature faster. This is one of the most well-established, evidence-backed treatments in obstetrics, typically given between 24 and 34 weeks, which covers most of where you are right now.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'These injections work by speeding up a natural process your baby\'s lungs are already undergoing, helping the tiny air sacs mature enough to function better if birth happens sooner than expected. Research consistently shows this genuinely reduces breathing problems and other complications in babies born preterm, particularly when timed roughly two to seven days before delivery.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'This is only given when there\'s a real, specific reason to think preterm birth is likely soon, not as a routine precaution for every pregnancy at this stage. Whether this extends usefully to babies born a little later, closer to 34 to 36 weeks, is still being actively studied, and practice on this specific point varies.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor directly if you show any signs of preterm labour, since timing genuinely matters for how much benefit this treatment offers.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There\'s something genuinely remarkable about medicine that can, in effect, buy your baby\'s lungs a little more time to catch up, right when it matters most.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Given when preterm birth is genuinely expected within about a week.',
        'Typically offered between 24 and 34 weeks of pregnancy.',
        'Helps your baby\'s lungs mature faster, reducing breathing complications.',
        'Works best when timed roughly 2 to 7 days before delivery.',
        'Only given for a specific reason, not as routine precaution.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-116',
      'PREG-HEALTH-096',
      'PREG-HEALTH-066',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-118',
    number: 352,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Is a baby born at 34 to 36 weeks ("late preterm") actually at risk?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 to 9 (weeks 34-36)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Somewhat, yes, though genuinely less than babies born earlier, and the overall outlook remains reassuring. Late preterm babies can look deceptively similar to full-term babies, only smaller, but they\'re still meaningfully more likely to need some help with breathing, feeding, or temperature regulation than a baby born at full term.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Late preterm babies represent the large majority of all premature births, and the final few weeks of pregnancy that they miss, even though they seem minor, are genuinely important for finishing lung, brain, and metabolic maturity. This is exactly why doctors avoid delivering early without a genuine medical reason, even by just a week or two, whenever it\'s safe to continue the pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If your doctor is considering an early delivery for a specific medical reason at this stage, that decision weighs the benefit of delivering against these genuine, if generally manageable, late-preterm risks. Most late preterm babies do very well, often needing only brief monitoring or short-term support rather than intensive care, especially with good hospital facilities.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor to explain the specific reasoning if early delivery is ever discussed for you at this stage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Late preterm babies often look ready for the world, and in many ways they are; it\'s simply worth knowing they may need a little extra support finishing what those final weeks would have completed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Late preterm babies face somewhat higher risk than full-term babies.',
        'They represent the large majority of all premature births.',
        'The final weeks of pregnancy are genuinely important for finishing maturity.',
        'Doctors avoid early delivery without genuine medical reason for this reason.',
        'Most late preterm babies do very well with good hospital care.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-115',
      'PREG-HEALTH-117',
      'PREG-HEALTH-116',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BIRTHPREP-005',
    number: 353,
    month: 8,
    question: 'How do I choose where to deliver, and what should I check about the hospital in advance?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Look beyond a hospital\'s reputation or brand name to specifics: whether a Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) is on-site rather than requiring transfer elsewhere, whether your own doctor delivers there personally or you\'ll see whoever\'s on call, and whether emergency C-section and anaesthesia are genuinely available around the clock, not just during standard hours.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'A hospital without an on-site Neonatal Intensive Care Unit can mean your baby is transferred elsewhere in an emergency, which costs precious time exactly when it matters most; this single factor is worth prioritising over almost anything else. Similarly, "24/7 availability" sometimes means on-call staff who need time to arrive, rather than someone already present, which is a meaningfully different level of readiness in a genuine emergency.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Visit in person if you can, rather than relying only on a website or a friend\'s recommendation, and ask to actually see the labour rooms and the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit rather than just being told about them. Ask directly what a typical delivery package includes, and what gets billed separately, since costs can add up in ways that aren\'t obvious upfront.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your own doctor which hospitals they actually deliver at, and how the practical reality there matches their answers to your specific questions.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A polished lobby tells you far less than a straight answer to "can I see your Neonatal Intensive Care Unit"; trust the direct answers over the marketing.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Prioritise an on-site Neonatal Intensive Care Unit over almost anything else.',
        'Ask whether your doctor delivers there personally, or you\'ll see whoever\'s on call.',
        'Confirm genuine round-the-clock emergency C-section and anaesthesia availability.',
        'Visit in person and ask to see the labour rooms and Neonatal Intensive Care Unit directly.',
        'Get a clear picture of what\'s included in the delivery package versus billed separately.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BIRTHPREP-003',
      'PREG-PARTNER-006',
      'PREG-HEALTH-116',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-PARTNER-008',
    number: 354,
    month: 8,
    question: 'How do I involve my partner or family in birth planning, given Indian hospital norms?',
    category: 'Partner & Relationships',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Start conversations early, since Indian hospital norms genuinely vary on how involved a partner or family member can be, and it\'s better to align expectations well before labour than to navigate this for the first time under pressure. Share your birth plan and hospital\'s specific policies with them directly, rather than assuming everyone already knows what to expect.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Many Indian families are used to extended family being closely involved throughout pregnancy, yet hospital policy at the actual moment of delivery is often more restrictive than that broader involvement suggests, one companion, sometimes only during certain parts of labour. This gap between family expectation and hospital reality is exactly where confusion and frustration tend to surface, which is why setting expectations in advance matters so much.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Have a direct conversation with your partner and close family about what your specific hospital actually allows, and who you\'d like as your one companion if that\'s the limit. It can help to designate one family member as the point of contact for updates to others waiting outside, so your partner isn\'t managing multiple people\'s expectations while also supporting you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'This is a family conversation more than a medical one, though your hospital\'s maternity ward can confirm the specific policy details worth sharing with your family in advance.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A little advance clarity here saves a lot of friction later; everyone showing up with the same understanding of what the day will actually look like.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Indian hospital norms on family involvement vary considerably; check yours specifically.',
        'Share your birth plan and hospital policy with family well before labour.',
        'One companion is often the limit, even when family involvement is otherwise close.',
        'Designate one family member as the point of contact for updates to others.',
        'Setting expectations early prevents confusion and friction on the day itself.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-PARTNER-006',
      'PREG-PARTNER-007',
      'PREG-BIRTHPREP-002',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-015',
    number: 355,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Why do I feel a mix of impatience and fear as my due date gets closer?',
    category: 'Mental & Emotional Wellbeing',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'This particular mix, wanting it to be over and feeling nervous about what "over" actually involves, is extremely common as your due date approaches. Both feelings are genuinely allowed to exist at once; neither cancels the other out.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Physical discomfort by this stage is real and mounting, which naturally fuels impatience for it to be done, while the unknowns of labour and early parenthood, however much you\'ve read about them, remain genuinely unknown until you\'re living them. Your mind is essentially trying to hold two true things simultaneously: this is hard and I want it to end, and I\'m not entirely sure I\'m ready for what comes next.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Naming both feelings out loud, to your partner, a friend, or even just to yourself, tends to help more than trying to talk yourself into feeling only one or the other. Staying connected to practical preparation, your hospital bag, your birth plan, your questions for your doctor, can genuinely help the fear feel more manageable, since it channels nervous energy into something useful.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention it if this feeling starts affecting your sleep, appetite, or daily functioning in a significant way; support beyond simply pushing through is available.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Wanting your pregnancy to end and feeling unsure about what\'s next aren\'t contradictions; they\'re simply both true, and most women carry exactly this mix in these final weeks.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Feeling both impatient and afraid as your due date nears is extremely common.',
        'Physical discomfort fuels impatience; genuine unknowns fuel the fear.',
        'Both feelings are allowed to exist at once; neither cancels the other out.',
        'Naming both feelings out loud tends to help more than suppressing one.',
        'Practical preparation can help channel nervous energy usefully.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-EMOTION-014',
      'PREG-BODY-062',
      'PREG-BIRTHPREP-002',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BIRTHPREP-006',
    number: 356,
    month: 8,
    question: 'How do I get my baby used to the car seat?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (arrange before delivery; relevant through early newborn weeks)',
    canI: 'Do',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Most babies take some time to settle into a car seat, and crying there in the early weeks is genuinely common, not a sign anything is wrong. Starting with short, calm practice sessions at home, before you actually need it for a trip, tends to help the most.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'A car seat asks something new of a newborn: being strapped in facing away from you, without the constant physical contact they\'re used to from being held, which can feel unsettling at first simply because it\'s unfamiliar. Motion, noise, and losing sight of your face can add to this, especially in the very early weeks before your baby has had much chance to get used to any of it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Let your baby sit in the car seat for short, relaxed periods at home first, so it becomes familiar before it\'s ever attached to an actual journey. Before a real trip, make sure your baby is fed, freshly changed, and dressed comfortably, since a hungry or uncomfortable baby will struggle regardless of the seat itself. A small mirror positioned so you can see each other, a soft toy attached to the seat, and keeping the car appropriately cool can all help too. Try to start actual trips when your baby is already calm or sleepy, rather than mid-fuss, and know that most babies do settle into it with a bit of consistent practice.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention persistent, extreme distress specifically in the car seat to your paediatrician, since in rare cases this can be linked to something like reflux discomfort worth checking.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This particular struggle is almost universally shared among new parents; it usually isn\'t about the seat itself, but about a genuinely new experience your baby simply needs time to adjust to.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Crying in the car seat during early weeks is common, not a sign of a problem.',
        'Short, calm practice sessions at home help build familiarity first.',
        'A fed, changed, comfortably dressed baby handles the seat better.',
        'A mirror, a soft toy, and a cool car can all genuinely help.',
        'Mention persistent, extreme distress to your paediatrician, since reflux can sometimes be a factor.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BIRTHPREP-004',
      'PREG-BIRTHPREP-001',
      'PREG-BABY-025',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-119',
    number: 357,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What is a membrane sweep, and does it actually help start labour?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 9 (typically offered 38-40 weeks, close to due date)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A membrane sweep, sometimes called a stretch and sweep, is when your doctor inserts one or two gloved fingers through your cervix and gently separates the membranes surrounding your baby from the lower part of your womb. The honest picture: a large recent Indian trial found it genuinely reduced the need for formal induction, while a global Cochrane review describes the overall evidence as low certainty. Both are legitimate findings from different bodies of research.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'This gentle separation is thought to release hormones called prostaglandins locally, which can help ripen your cervix and encourage labour to start on its own. A 2026 trial at a Delhi hospital found that women who had repeated sweeps needed formal induction far less often than those who didn\'t, about 29 percent versus 69 percent, and went into labour somewhat sooner. Set against this, the international Cochrane review, pooling many smaller and more varied studies, found the overall evidence for effectiveness was less certain, meaning results have been genuinely mixed across different settings and populations.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: it\'s usually offered close to your due date, around 38 to 40 weeks, particularly if there\'s a reason to gently encourage labour rather than move straight to formal induction. It can cause some discomfort, spotting, or period-like cramping afterward, all normal and usually brief. Whether it works well for you individually is genuinely hard to predict from the research alone.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether this is being offered to you as an alternative to induction, and what to expect afterward.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth stating plainly: this isn\'t a guaranteed shortcut, and the evidence genuinely varies by source; it\'s a reasonable, low-risk option to discuss with your doctor near your due date, not something to request instead of medical guidance.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A membrane sweep gently separates the membranes from the lower womb by hand.',
        'A recent Indian trial found it meaningfully reduced the need for formal induction.',
        'A global Cochrane review describes the overall evidence as low certainty.',
        'Usually offered around 38 to 40 weeks, close to your due date.',
        'Mild spotting or cramping afterward is normal and usually brief.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-086',
      'PREG-HEALTH-097',
      'PREG-HEALTH-083',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-120',
    number: 358,
    month: 8,
    question: 'What are Braxton Hicks contractions?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (can begin second trimester)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Braxton Hicks contractions, sometimes called "practice contractions" or "false labour," are a tightening of your womb that comes and goes, without opening your cervix or leading to actual birth. They can start as early as the second trimester but are most noticeable in the third.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'These contractions are thought to help tone your womb\'s muscle and may also improve blood flow to your placenta, a kind of quiet rehearsal for the real thing. What sets them apart from true labour is mainly the pattern: Braxton Hicks are irregular, don\'t reliably get closer together or stronger over time, and often ease off if you change position, walk around, or simply rest. Common triggers include dehydration, a full bladder, physical activity, or your baby moving.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Staying hydrated and changing position often genuinely helps if these become uncomfortable. Getting familiar with how they feel, a tightening or hardening across your belly that comes and goes, helps you tell them apart from real contractions later, which tend to be more regular, more painful, and get steadily closer together.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor if you\'re before 37 weeks and contractions become regular, or if they\'re accompanied by bleeding, fluid leaking, or pelvic pressure.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Named after the doctor who first accurately described them over a century ago, these are simply your womb\'s way of practising for the real event, nothing to worry about on their own.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Braxton Hicks are irregular tightenings that don\'t open the cervix or lead to birth.',
        'Can start in the second trimester, most noticeable in the third.',
        'They don\'t reliably get stronger or closer together over time, unlike real labour.',
        'Common triggers include dehydration, a full bladder, and physical activity.',
        'Regular contractions before 37 weeks need a call to your doctor.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-083',
      'PREG-HEALTH-066',
      'PREG-BODY-055',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-121',
    number: 359,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Why might doctors give a lung-maturity injection before a planned C-section, even if I\'m not in preterm labour?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 to 9 (relevant to planned C-sections before 39 weeks)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (medication-adjacent, delivery-mode content, per Escalation Guardrail)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Even a caesarean section (C-section) planned for close to your due date, before labour starts on its own, carries a somewhat higher chance of your baby having temporary breathing difficulty than a vaginal birth does. In some cases, doctors give a course of lung-maturity injections beforehand to help reduce this, though the evidence on how much it actually helps at this later stage is genuinely mixed, and practice varies by hospital.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Babies born by planned C-section before labour begins miss out on hormonal and physical changes that happen during labour itself, changes that help clear fluid from their lungs and prepare them to breathe air. This is why respiratory issues are somewhat more common after a planned C-section done before 39 weeks specifically. Some clinical trials have found that giving this injection before such a C-section meaningfully reduces the chance of breathing problems and Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) admission; others, including a more recent study in an Asian population, found no significant difference. This is genuinely not standard practice everywhere, including in some countries, precisely because the evidence remains mixed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: if your doctor recommends this for a planned C-section before 39 weeks, it\'s a reasonable, evidence-informed option worth discussing, but it isn\'t a guaranteed protection, and plenty of good hospitals don\'t offer it routinely at this stage without a specific reason. Ask what your hospital\'s usual practice is and why.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether this applies to your planned delivery timing, and what their reasoning is either way.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth stating plainly: this is one of those areas where thoughtful doctors genuinely disagree, which is worth knowing so you don\'t read a hospital\'s choice either way as a red flag.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Planned C-sections before labour starts carry a somewhat higher breathing-difficulty risk.',
        'This is because babies miss labour\'s own lung-fluid-clearing changes.',
        'Some trials show a real benefit from lung-maturity injections here; others show none.',
        'This isn\'t standard practice everywhere, given the genuinely mixed evidence.',
        'Ask your doctor about their hospital\'s usual practice and reasoning.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-117',
      'PREG-HEALTH-078',
      'PREG-HEALTH-122',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-122',
    number: 360,
    month: 8,
    question: 'At what point would my baby need to stay in the NICU (Neonatal Intensive Care Unit)?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 (weeks 32-35)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'There\'s no single sharp cutoff; it depends more on your baby\'s actual condition at birth than on gestational age alone. That said, babies born before about 34 to 35 weeks are routinely admitted for at least some monitoring, while babies born closer to term usually only need the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) if a specific issue comes up.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Doctors watch for a few key things right after birth: whether your baby is breathing comfortably on their own, whether they can maintain their body temperature, whether they\'re able to feed effectively, and whether their blood sugar and other basic measures are stable. Struggling with any of these, more likely the earlier a baby is born, is what actually triggers Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) admission, not the gestational age number by itself. This is exactly why two babies born at the same number of weeks can have quite different outcomes.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If your baby is born earlier than full term, ask your care team directly what specific things they\'re monitoring for and what would prompt a Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) stay in your baby\'s case. Many babies born even a few weeks early do fine with just brief observation rather than an extended stay.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor what applies to your specific situation if an earlier delivery is ever discussed for you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It\'s less about a due-date calculator and more about how your particular baby is actually doing in those first hours; that\'s genuinely the more accurate way to think about it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'There\'s no single sharp gestational-age cutoff for NICU admission.',
        'Babies born before about 34 to 35 weeks are routinely monitored at minimum.',
        'Breathing, temperature control, feeding, and blood sugar are the key things checked.',
        'A baby\'s actual condition at birth matters more than the week number alone.',
        'Ask your care team what specifically applies to your baby if born early.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-116',
      'PREG-HEALTH-115',
      'PREG-HEALTH-118',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-123',
    number: 361,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Does an epidural always work, and what happens if it doesn\'t fully relieve my pain?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (birth planning)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'No, not always, and it\'s worth knowing this honestly rather than expecting complete relief every time. Roughly 1 in 10 epidurals doesn\'t work as well as expected, most often as patchy relief, good on one side but not the other, rather than no relief at all. There are established, effective fixes for this.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'This can happen for a few reasons: the catheter (the thin tube delivering medication) may have shifted slightly out of place, the dose may need adjusting, or the medication may simply be spreading unevenly. This isn\'t a sign anything went wrong with your body or the procedure; it\'s a genuinely common, well-understood issue that anaesthetists are experienced at troubleshooting.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you notice pain returning or relief feeling uneven, tell your nurse or anaesthetist right away rather than assuming it\'s just how epidurals feel. Common fixes include adjusting your position, giving an extra dose (called a top-up), or, less often, replacing the catheter entirely, and most cases genuinely improve with one of these simple steps.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Speak up immediately if your pain relief feels incomplete or uneven; this is a routine, expected part of managing an epidural, not an unusual complaint.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'An imperfect epidural isn\'t a failure of the procedure or of you; it\'s simply a fixable hiccup that your care team has seen many times before.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'About 1 in 10 epidurals don\'t fully work as expected, often as patchy relief.',
        'This usually comes from catheter position or medication spread, not anything you did.',
        'Speak up immediately if relief feels incomplete or uneven.',
        'Common fixes include repositioning, a top-up dose, or replacing the catheter.',
        'Most cases genuinely improve once your team adjusts things.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-098',
      'PREG-HEALTH-100',
      'PREG-HEALTH-101',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BIRTHPREP-007',
    number: 362,
    month: 8,
    question: 'How does hospital registration and booking work for delivery in India?',
    category: 'Labour & Birth Prep',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward (register around weeks 28-32)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Most private Indian hospitals want you to register for delivery well in advance, often from around 28 to 32 weeks, especially if you want a specific room category or a particular doctor. This usually involves choosing a delivery package, submitting basic documents, and sometimes paying a booking amount to secure your bed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Hospitals manage a limited number of beds and doctors, which is exactly why early registration genuinely matters for getting your preferred room type or doctor\'s availability, particularly around your expected delivery window. This process differs meaningfully between vaginal delivery and a C-section mainly in package pricing and expected stay length, roughly 2 to 3 days for a vaginal delivery versus 4 to 5 days for a C-section, since a package quote assumes one or the other.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Ask specifically what\'s included in your package, room charges, doctor\'s fee, tests, medicines, and what gets billed separately, since this varies considerably between hospitals. Get everything in writing, and ask directly what happens to package pricing if your delivery type changes partway, for instance if a planned vaginal delivery becomes an emergency C-section.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'This is largely an administrative process, but ask your doctor which hospitals they\'re affiliated with and by when you should register there.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Getting the paperwork and package details sorted early means one less thing to think about later, precisely when you\'ll want to be focused on other things.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Register around 28 to 32 weeks for your preferred room and doctor.',
        'Involves choosing a package, submitting documents, and often a booking amount.',
        'Vaginal delivery and C-section packages differ mainly in stay length and pricing.',
        'Ask what\'s included versus billed separately, and get it in writing.',
        'Ask what happens to pricing if your delivery type changes partway through.',
      ]),
