        'Private hospitals in India commonly ask for a partial advance, sometimes a few thousand rupees to secure a bed, sometimes a larger deposit closer to your expected package cost, particularly for a planned C-section. This is a genuinely legitimate business practice in most cases, but it\'s also an area where a few hospitals have overstepped, so it\'s worth knowing your position clearly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Hospitals ask for advances to secure your bed and confirm your booking, similar to any advance deposit for a scheduled service, and many will adjust this amount against your final bill rather than charging it separately. Where real concern exists: in a genuine medical emergency, admission for essential, life-saving treatment should not be refused or delayed purely over an unpaid deposit, and in several states, charitable and trust hospitals are specifically barred by regulation from demanding an advance payment before providing emergency care. In practice, this rule isn\'t always followed, and disputes over this do happen.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: for planned, non-emergency registration, a reasonable advance is normal and worth budgeting for early; ask exactly what it covers and get a receipt. For a genuine emergency, know that you have a right to essential care regardless of immediate payment at many hospitals, particularly charitable ones, even if this isn\'t always how things play out in the moment. Keep some funds accessible in the final weeks specifically for this, since needing to arrange money quickly during an emergency adds stress you don\'t need.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'This is a hospital administration matter, but ask your hospital directly, in writing if possible, what their advance payment policy is for both a planned and an emergency situation.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth naming plainly: knowing your rights here doesn\'t guarantee smooth treatment from every hospital, but it does mean you\'re not navigating this blind if a difficult situation ever comes up.',
      ], []),
