  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-029',
    number: 365,
    month: 8,
    question: 'Is there a specific number of movements I should feel each day, and when exactly should I go to the hospital?',
    category: 'Baby Development',
    applicability: 'Month 8 onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes',
    held: true,
    heldNote: 'Yes, holds for Ishaan\'s sign-off (reduced movement is safety-critical content, per Escalation Guardrail; genuine international guideline disagreement worth your specific review)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Honestly, major medical bodies genuinely disagree on this. Some guidelines, including in Canada, support formal daily counting toward a specific number. Others, including in the United Kingdom, the United States, and the World Health Organization\'s own guidance, don\'t recommend rigid daily counting as a proven screening tool, and instead emphasise knowing your baby\'s individual pattern. Where there\'s much clearer agreement: once you notice a genuine reduction, act on it promptly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Large studies on formal fetal movement counting have produced mixed, sometimes contradictory results, which is exactly why practice varies so much by country. What\'s much more consistently supported is the response once a woman perceives reduced movement, regardless of whether she\'s been formally counting: lying on your left side, focusing on movement for about 2 hours, and contacting your doctor promptly if you feel fewer than 10 movements in that time, or if anything about the pattern feels different from what\'s normal for your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Our honest take: you don\'t need to count religiously every day for it to count as good care, but you do need to stay genuinely familiar with your own baby\'s pattern, since that awareness is what actually protects you either way. If in doubt at any point, the safe, always-correct move is simply to check with your doctor, not to wait and reassess later.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor promptly for any genuine change in your baby\'s usual movement pattern, whether or not you\'ve been formally counting.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A caution worth stating plainly: when medical bodies themselves disagree on the method, the safest approach is simple, trust a genuine change when you feel it, and call rather than wait to see.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Major guidelines genuinely disagree on formal daily movement counting.',
        'Some countries support it; others emphasise knowing your own pattern instead.',
        'There\'s much clearer agreement on what to do once movement feels reduced.',
        'The standard response: 2 hours on your left side, call if fewer than 10 movements.',
        'When in doubt, call your doctor rather than waiting to reassess.',
      ]),
